---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import StatusPill from '../../../components/StatusPill.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../../lib/siteUrl';
---

<Layout
  title="Frame Interpolation — Varve Docs"
  description="Use Varve's experimental local RIFE workflow to generate a derived in-between image from two photographs."
>
  <div class="container-custom">
    <article class="doc-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/docs')}>Docs</a> / Frame Interpolation
      </nav>

      <StatusPill status="experimental" />
      <h1>Frame Interpolation</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Generate a new in-between raster image from two selected photographs with the optional local
        RIFE model. This is an image-generation utility, not a layer-compositing or blend-mode tool.
      </p>

      <h2>When to use it</h2>
      <p>
        Choose Frame Interpolation when two images are consecutive or closely related frames and you
        need a derived midpoint for a motion study, transition, or retiming experiment. It does not
        merge the source layers, preserve their separate masks, or add a blend mode between siblings.
      </p>

      <h2>Workflow</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Select one image-filled shape in the Photo workspace.</li>
        <li>Open <strong>Adjustments → Frame Interpolation</strong> and choose the second image.</li>
        <li>Download the optional model when prompted. The model is stored locally on this device.</li>
        <li>Select <strong>Interpolate</strong> to generate a preview, then choose <strong>Apply</strong> to create a new derived image layer.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Source and result behavior</h2>
      <p>
        The two source images remain unchanged and undoable. Applying the operation inserts a new
        PNG-backed image node; it does not replace either source, attach an Object Filter, or create
        a timeline keyframe. Canceling a generation leaves the document untouched.
      </p>

      <h2>Limitations</h2>
      <p>
        This workflow is experimental. RIFE output quality depends on the relationship between the
        two images, and the shipped channel convention has not been validated against a reference
        runtime. Large images can require substantial memory. Saved documents contain the derived
        result and do not require the model to display it later, but regenerating the result requires
        the model and an available local inference provider.
      </p>

      <p>
        For photographic corrections use <a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/image-treatments')}>Image Treatments</a>.
        For actual layer compositing use Object Filters, masks, and Adjustment Layers.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .doc-page { max-width: 760px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 3rem 0 5rem; }
  .breadcrumb { font-size: 0.875rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .breadcrumb a { color: var(--text-secondary); }
  h1 { color: var(--text-primary); font: 700 clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2.75rem)/1.1 var(--font-editorial); margin: 1rem 0; }
  .lead { color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: 1.2rem; line-height: 1.75; margin-bottom: 2.5rem; }
  h2 { color: var(--text-primary); margin-top: 2.5rem; }
  p, li { color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.75; }
  li + li { margin-top: 0.65rem; }
  strong { color: var(--text-primary); }
  a { color: var(--text-link); }
</style>
